00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:34.140
Everyone talks about giving agents memory as if it is just stuffing conversation history into a vector

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:41.100
store. In practice, it requires careful separation between short-term working memory, long-term

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:48.000
episodic memory, and user-specific facts. Without that separation, the model starts hallucinating

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:55.040
stale context. The core move is inserting a thin middleware layer that intercepts every model call.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:06:02.320
It retrieves relevant memories by session and user ID. Then injects them into the prompt.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:10.040
This adds only a small constant overhead per request. It removes the need to resend entire histories,

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:17.520
which matters one session span days or multiple users. The common mistake is treating memory as one

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:23.580
undifferentiated store. Without explicit scoping, you quickly pollute the context with irrelevant

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:31.100
past interactions. Start with a simple key value layer, scoped by user and session. Add retrieval

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:46.940
only when context length or task complexity justifies the extra latency. If you have not tried

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:52.900
paper pairs, see whether the long context improvements actually translate to your domain.

